#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a681cf28-41fa-5547-ba25-ee6a720e1a4f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "7302747a-df6d-5aa9-8118-c9e5f3b1574d" :question/math-academy-id "e-6192" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $5\\sin x = -7\\cos x$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\sin x &= -7\\cos x \\\\\n\\frac{5\\sin x}{\\cos x} &= -\\frac{7\\cos x}{\\cos x} \\\\\n\\frac{5\\sin x}{\\cos x} &= -\\frac{7\\cos x}{\\cos x} \\\\\n5 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} &= -7 \\\\\n5\\tan x &= -7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x$:\n\n$$\n\\tan x = -\\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nThe length of the interval for $x$ is $2\\pi$, so we will likely require $2$ solutions.\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x$:\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{5}\\right) \\approx -54.46\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the domain\n\n$$\n0^\\circ \\leq x < 360^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo get a value that's inside the domain, we add $180^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= \\arctan (-\\frac{7}{5}) + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 54.46^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 125.54^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution inside the given interval, we add $180^\\circ$ to $x_1$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx 125.54^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 305.54^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are\n\n$$\nx= 126^\\circ, 306^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092034 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf1a53c0-e652-5732-9d5a-5aad004c2676" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "072d7328-7c31-5d09-b2d3-91b564624b62" :question/math-academy-id "e-6196" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees to the nearest degree, the solutions to the equation $3\\sin x + 12\\cos x = 0$ for $-180^\\circ < x \\leq 180^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "We divide each term of the equation by $\\cos x$ and use the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\sin x + 12\\cos x &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{3\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{12\\cos x}{\\cos x} &= 0 \\\\\n\\frac{3\\sin x}{\\cos x} + \\frac{12\\cos x}{\\cos x} &= 0 \\\\\n3 \\cdot \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} + 12 &= 0 \\\\\n3\\tan x + 12 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\tan x &= -12 \\\\\n\\tan x &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x$:\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan(-4) \\approx -75.96^\\circ\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\nx_1 = -75.96^\\circ\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we *add* $180^\\circ$. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&\\approx - 75.96^{∘} + 180^{∘} \\\\\n&= 104.04^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions, rounded to the nearest degree, are\n\n$$\nx= -76^\\circ, 104^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf1a53c0-e652-5732-9d5a-5aad004c2676" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Using the Sin-Cos-Tan Identity Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5b0272d-8eed-5f4d-b5b3-6b329d4f575e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7π^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091476} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fa19fe-6805-57f3-af01-4fbf8dc1db3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π^{2}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091477} {:answer/id #uuid "1b92f980-c7d4-5710-b20b-64f9b024e034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5π^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091478} {:answer/id #uuid "224e62b7-54d3-5eff-a1e4-4c606e1e5a41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091479} {:answer/id #uuid "7c60c279-ff47-5a7c-8817-eea4376bf832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{π^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b92f980-c7d4-5710-b20b-64f9b024e034" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5π^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091478} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b307dce-83c1-5dc6-b94a-9680cbb09d46" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186091475}] :question/id #uuid "40bdf0c4-a9af-5a19-bf92-60c84db76070" :question/math-academy-id "q-8876" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nGiven that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $3\\sin x - \\sqrt{3}\\cos x = 0$ for $- π < x \\le π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$?" :question/requires-calculator true}]}
